 "brief": "## 1. The work\nA Latin prose handbook of lay Christian devotion by Desiderius Erasmus, cast as a letter of spiritual counsel built on an extended military metaphor. This printing adds an editor's dedicatory letter, commendatory Latin verses, a subject index and printed sidenotes. The register is learned humanist Latin, rich in classical allusion and scriptural citation. Translate it as the base text.\n\n## 2. Glossary\nEnchiridion — Enchiridion\nmiles christianus — Christian soldier\nmilitia — warfare\nimperator — commander\ndux — captain\nhostis — enemy\narma — arms\nAgonothetes — president of the games\ncaro — flesh\nspiritus — spirit\nanima — soul\nanimus — mind\nmundus — world\nvitium — vice\nvirtus — virtue\npeccatum — sin\ndaemon — demon\ncerimonia — ceremony\ncharitas — charity\npietas — piety\nreligio — religion\nsuperstitio — superstition\nmonachus — monk\nprecatio — prayer\noratio — prayer\nscientia — knowledge\nsapientia — wisdom\nscriptura sacra — Holy Scripture\ntypus — type\nmystice — in the mystical sense\n\n## 3. Layout\nPrefatory pages hold a dedication, verse (keep the lines as lines), and an alphabetical index giving Roman-numeral folio numbers. Text pages carry a running head (\"Folium II\"), signatures and catchwords; omit these. Translate the printed sidenotes as [Margin: …] at the point where each falls. Mark handwritten annotations as [Ms. note: …]. Expand abbreviations silently. Keep Greek words and add the English in brackets.\n\n## 4. Names\nErasmus Roterodamus — Erasmus of Rotterdam\nHieronymus Emser — Hieronymus Emser\nErnestus de Schlepnitz — Ernst von Schleinitz\nChristus / Iesus — Christ / Jesus\nMoses — Moses\nIob — Job\nPaulus — Paul\nPlato — Plato\nOrigenes — Origen\nAugustinus — Augustine\nAegyptus — Egypt\nAeua / Eua — Eve\nIacob, Esau — Jacob, Esau\nMaro — Virgil\nHercules — Hercules",
